{
  "ResearchDossier": {
    "topic": "milk first or tea first",
    "summary": "George Orwell's 1946 essay 'A Nice Cup of Tea' lists eleven golden rules; he calls milk-or-tea-first 'one of the most controversial points of all' and insists on tea first so you can regulate the milk. In June 2003, for his centenary, the Royal Society of Chemistry issued 'How to make a Perfect Cup of Tea', with Loughborough chemical engineer Dr Andrew Stapley: pour milk in FIRST, because milk added to hot tea heats unevenly and its proteins denature. The same guide warms the pot in a microwave.",
    "angle": "Orwell said tea first. The chemists said milk first.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "id": "c1",
        "text": "Orwell's essay 'A Nice Cup of Tea' was first published in the London Evening Standard on 12 January 1946 and gives eleven rules he regarded as golden.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Nice_Cup_of_Tea",
            "title": "A Nice Cup of Tea - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "Here are my own eleven rules, every one of which I regard as golden."
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c2",
        "text": "Orwell calls milk-or-tea-first 'one of the most controversial points of all', saying every family in Britain probably has two schools of thought.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Nice_Cup_of_Tea",
            "title": "A Nice Cup of Tea - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "one of the most controversial points of all"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c3",
        "text": "Orwell put tea first because one can exactly regulate the amount of milk.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Nice_Cup_of_Tea",
            "title": "A Nice Cup of Tea - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "one can exactly regulate the amount of milk"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c4",
        "text": "In June 2003, to mark Orwell's centenary, the Royal Society of Chemistry released 'How to make a Perfect Cup of Tea'.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.hmssurprise.org/sites/default/files/pictures/lissunsgalley/tea.pdf",
            "title": "Royal Society of Chemistry news release: How to make a Perfect Cup of Tea (2003)",
            "quote": "How to make a Perfect Cup of Tea"
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/world/science-solves-issue-of-milk-or-tea-first/NH2JVCYN5XZN2GSKWYMA42SNQA",
            "title": "Science solves issue of milk or tea first - NZ Herald, 25 June 2003",
            "quote": "to coincide with the centenary of his birth"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c5",
        "text": "The guide says: 'Pour milk into the cup FIRST, followed by the tea.'",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.hmssurprise.org/sites/default/files/pictures/lissunsgalley/tea.pdf",
            "title": "Royal Society of Chemistry news release: How to make a Perfect Cup of Tea (2003)",
            "quote": "Pour milk into the cup FIRST, followed by the tea"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c6",
        "text": "Dr Andrew Stapley of Loughborough University said milk added to hot tea is more likely to have its proteins denature, tasting slightly stale.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/world/science-solves-issue-of-milk-or-tea-first/NH2JVCYN5XZN2GSKWYMA42SNQA",
            "title": "Science solves issue of milk or tea first - NZ Herald, 25 June 2003",
            "quote": "denaturation of milk proteins"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c7",
        "text": "The guide says to warm the ceramic teapot with a little water in a microwave for one minute.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.hmssurprise.org/sites/default/files/pictures/lissunsgalley/tea.pdf",
            "title": "Royal Society of Chemistry news release: How to make a Perfect Cup of Tea (2003)",
            "quote": "in a microwave oven on full power for one minute"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      }
